            {
              "h": "Process Control Block (PCB) & CPU Switch Between Processes (Slides 3.9–3.11)",
              "pts": [
                "Each process is represented in the operating system by a <b>Process Control Block (PCB)</b> (also called a task control block).",
                "<b>Seven pieces of information stored in the PCB (Slide 3.9)</b>: (1) <b>Process state</b> (new, ready, running, waiting, halted); (2) <b>Program counter</b> (address of the next instruction to execute); (3) <b>CPU registers</b> (accumulators, index registers, stack pointers, general-purpose registers); (4) <b>CPU-scheduling information</b> (process priority, pointers to scheduling queues); (5) <b>Memory-management information</b> (base/limit registers, page tables, or segment tables); (6) <b>Accounting information</b> (amount of CPU and real time used, time limits, process numbers); (7) <b>I/O status information</b> (allocated I/O devices and list of open files).",
                "<b>CPU Switch From Process P0 to Process P1 (Slide 3.11)</b>: When an interrupt or system call occurs while <code>P0</code> is executing, the OS saves <code>P0</code>'s state into <code>PCB0</code>, reloads the saved state of <code>P1</code> from <code>PCB1</code>, and executes <code>P1</code>. When another interrupt or system call occurs, the OS saves <code>P1</code>'s state into <code>PCB1</code> and reloads <code>PCB0</code>."
              ]
            },
            {
              "h": "Process Scheduling Queues & Queuing Diagram (Slides 3.14–3.16)",
              "pts": [
                "To maximize CPU utilization (multiprogramming) and switch processes rapidly onto the CPU (time sharing), the <b>process scheduler</b> maintains three main types of <b>scheduling queues</b>:",
                "<b>1. Job queue</b>: Set of <b>all processes</b> in the system (including those waiting on disk to be brought into memory).",
                "<b>2. Ready queue</b>: Set of all processes residing in <b>main memory</b> that are ready and waiting to execute (stored as a linked list of PCBs with a header containing <code>head</code> and <code>tail</code> pointers).",
                "<b>3. Device queues</b>: Set of processes waiting for a particular I/O device (e.g., magnetic tape unit 0/1, disk unit 0, terminal unit 0).",
                "<b>Queuing Diagram Flows (Slide 3.16)</b>: Once dispatched to the CPU, a process may: (a) issue an <b>I/O request</b> and enter an I/O queue; (b) have its <b>time slice expire</b> and return directly to the ready queue; (c) <b>fork a child</b> and wait for the child to execute; or (d) <b>wait for an interrupt</b> before returning to the ready queue."
              ]
            },
            {
              "h": "Long-Term, Short-Term & Medium-Term Schedulers and Context Switch (Slides 3.17–3.21)",
              "pts": [
                "<b>Long-term scheduler (Job scheduler)</b>: Selects which processes from the job pool should be brought into the <b>ready queue</b> in memory. It is invoked <b>infrequently</b> (seconds or minutes, so it may be slow) and directly controls the <b>degree of multiprogramming</b> (the number of processes in memory).",
                "<b>Short-term scheduler (CPU scheduler)</b>: Selects which process from the ready queue should execute next and allocates the CPU to it. It is invoked <b>very frequently</b> (every few milliseconds) and therefore <b>must be very fast</b>.",
                "<b>I/O-bound vs. CPU-bound Processes (Slide 3.18)</b>: An <b>I/O-bound process</b> spends more time doing I/O than computations and has <b>many very short CPU bursts</b>. A <b>CPU-bound process</b> spends more time doing computations and has <b>few very long CPU bursts</b>. The long-term scheduler strives for a balanced <b>process mix</b> of I/O-bound and CPU-bound jobs (if all processes are I/O-bound, the ready queue is nearly empty; if all are CPU-bound, the I/O waiting queues are nearly empty).",
                "<b>Medium-term scheduler (Slide 3.19)</b>: Added when the degree of multiprogramming needs to decrease. It removes partially executed processes from memory and stores them on disk (<b>swap out</b>), and later brings them back into memory (<b>swap in</b>) to continue execution—a scheme known as <b>swapping</b>.",
                "<b>Context Switch (Slide 3.21)</b>: Saving the state of the old process into its PCB and loading the saved state of the new process from its PCB. Context-switch time is <b>pure overhead</b> because the system does no useful work while switching; its speed depends on OS/PCB complexity and hardware support (such as multiple register sets)."
              ],
              "table": [
                [
                  "Scheduler Type",
                  "Also Called",
                  "Selection Role",
                  "Invocation Frequency",
                  "Effect on Multiprogramming"
                ],
                [
                  "<b>Long-Term</b>",
                  "Job Scheduler",
                  "Selects processes from disk pool to load into Ready Queue",
                  "Infrequent (seconds/minutes)",
                  "Increases / controls degree of multiprogramming"
                ],
                [
                  "<b>Short-Term</b>",
                  "CPU Scheduler",
                  "Selects ready process to execute next on the CPU",
                  "Very frequent (milliseconds)",
                  "Does not change degree of multiprogramming"
                ],
                [
                  "<b>Medium-Term</b>",
                  "Swapper",
                  "Swaps partially executed processes out to disk and back in",
                  "Intermediate (as needed)",
                  "Decreases degree of multiprogramming"
                ]
              ]
            },
            {
              "h": "Operations on Processes: Creation (fork/exec) & Termination (exit/abort) (Slides 3.22–3.28)",
              "pts": [
                "<b>Process Tree & PID</b>: A parent process creates child processes, which in turn create other processes, forming a tree of processes identified by a unique integer <b>process identifier (pid)</b>. In Linux (Slide 3.24), <code>init</code> (<code>pid = 1</code>) is the root parent of all user processes (such as <code>login</code>, <code>bash</code>, <code>sshd</code>) and <code>kthreadd</code> (<code>pid = 2</code>) manages kernel threads. In Google Chrome (Slide 3.22), each browser tab runs as a separate process for isolation.",
                "<b>Three Resource-Sharing Options (Slide 3.23)</b>: (1) Parent and children share <b>all</b> resources; (2) Children share a <b>subset</b> of parent's resources; (3) Parent and child share <b>no</b> resources.",
                "<b>Two Execution Options</b>: (1) Parent and children execute <b>concurrently</b>; (2) Parent <b>waits</b> until some or all of its children terminate.",
                "<b>Two Address-Space Options (Slide 3.25)</b>: (1) Child is a <b>duplicate</b> of the parent (has the same program and data); (2) Child has a <b>new program</b> loaded into it.",
                "<b>UNIX Process Creation (Slides 3.25–3.26)</b>: <code>fork()</code> creates a new child process that duplicates the parent's address space. <code>fork()</code> returns <code>0</code> to the newly created child process, returns the child's positive <code>pid (&gt; 0)</code> to the parent process, and returns <code>&lt; 0</code> if an error occurs. After <code>fork()</code>, the child can call <code>execlp()</code> (or <code>exec()</code>) to replace its memory space with a new program while the parent calls <code>wait(NULL)</code>.",
                "<b>Process Termination (Slide 3.28)</b>: A process finishes its last statement and calls <code>exit()</code>, returning its exit status to its parent via <code>wait()</code> while the OS deallocates its resources. A parent may terminate a child early via <code>abort()</code> for <b>three reasons</b>: (1) The child has <b>exceeded allocated resources</b>; (2) The <b>task assigned to the child is no longer required</b>; (3) The <b>parent is exiting</b> and the OS does not allow children to continue (<b>cascading termination</b>, initiated by the OS).",
                "<b>Zombie vs. Orphan Processes</b>: A <b>zombie process</b> is a process that has terminated, but whose parent has not yet called <code>wait()</code> (so its entry remains in the process table to hold its exit status). An <b>orphan process</b> is a child process whose parent terminated without invoking <code>wait()</code> (in UNIX/Linux, <code>init</code> (<code>pid = 1</code>) adopts orphan processes and periodically calls <code>wait()</code> on them)."
              ],
              "code": "#include <sys/types.h>\n#include <stdio.h>\n#include <unistd.h>\n\nint main() {\n    pid_t pid;\n    /* fork a child process */\n    pid = fork();\n    if (pid < 0) { /* error occurred */\n        fprintf(stderr, \"Fork Failed\");\n        return 1;\n    } else if (pid == 0) { /* child process */\n        execlp(\"/bin/ls\", \"ls\", NULL);\n    } else { /* parent process */\n        /* parent will wait for the child to complete */\n        wait(NULL);\n        printf(\"Child Complete\");\n    }\n    return 0;\n}"
            }
