            [
              "What is a process, what are the five states of a process, and what is a PCB? (Midterm 2017 Q3 & Midterm 2018 Q3)",
              "A <b>process</b> is a program in execution (an active entity in memory). A process's <b>state</b> is defined in part by the current activity of that process. Its five states are: (1) <b>new</b> (being created), (2) <b>ready</b> (in memory waiting to be assigned to a processor), (3) <b>running</b> (instructions are being executed), (4) <b>waiting</b> (waiting for an event such as I/O completion), and (5) <b>terminated</b> (finished execution). A <b>Process Control Block (PCB)</b> is the kernel data structure associated with each process that stores its process state, program counter, CPU registers, CPU-scheduling info, memory-management info, accounting info, and I/O status info."
            ],
            [
              "Describe the actions taken by a kernel to context-switch between processes. (Midterm 2018 Q4)",
              "When an interrupt or system call triggers a context switch from process <code>P0</code> to process <code>P1</code>: (1) The kernel saves the current CPU context of <code>P0</code> (program counter, processor registers, and process state) into <code>PCB0</code>; (2) The kernel updates <code>PCB0</code>'s state (e.g., to <code>ready</code> or <code>waiting</code>) and moves <code>PCB0</code> to the appropriate queue; (3) The kernel selects <code>P1</code>, reloads <code>P1</code>'s saved CPU context (registers, program counter, memory limits) from <code>PCB1</code>, updates <code>P1</code>'s state to <code>running</code>, and switches back to user mode to resume <code>P1</code>. Context-switch time is pure overhead."
            ],
            [
              "What is the difference between zombie and orphan processes? (Midterm 2019 v2 Q6)",
              "A <b>zombie process</b> is a child process that has finished execution (terminated via <code>exit()</code>), but whose parent has not yet called <code>wait()</code> to collect its exit status, so its PCB entry remains in the process table. An <b>orphan process</b> is a child process that is still executing after its parent process has already terminated without calling <code>wait()</code> (in UNIX/Linux, <code>init</code> with <code>pid = 1</code> adopts orphan processes)."
            ],
            [
              "Including the initial parent process, how many processes are created by three consecutive fork() calls? (Midterm 2019 v2 Q8)",
              "With 3 consecutive <code>fork()</code> calls, <b>2^3 = 8 processes</b> exist in total (1 initial parent process P0 plus 7 child processes). First <code>fork()</code> creates C1 (total 2); second <code>fork()</code> is executed by both P0 and C1 to create C2 and C3 (total 4); third <code>fork()</code> is executed by all 4 processes to create C4, C5, C6, and C7 (total 8)."
            ],
            [
              "Describe the differences among short-term, medium-term, and long-term scheduling. (Final 2017 Q3)",
              "<b>Long-term (job) scheduler</b> selects processes from the disk job pool to load into the ready queue in main memory; it runs infrequently (seconds/minutes) and controls the degree of multiprogramming. <b>Short-term (CPU) scheduler</b> selects which ready process in main memory gets the CPU next; it runs very frequently (milliseconds) and must be fast. <b>Medium-term scheduler</b> swaps partially executed processes out of main memory to disk (swap out) and back in (swap in) to decrease the degree of multiprogramming and free memory."
            ],
            [
              "When a process creates a new process, what are the possible options for execution, resource sharing, and termination? (Final 2019 Q4)",
              "<b>Execution options</b>: (1) Parent continues to execute concurrently with its children; (2) Parent waits until some or all children terminate. <b>Resource-sharing options</b>: (1) Parent and children share all resources; (2) Children share a subset of parent's resources; (3) Parent and child share no resources. <b>Reasons a parent aborts a child</b>: (1) Child exceeded allocated resources; (2) Task assigned to child is no longer required; (3) Parent is exiting and the OS enforces <b>cascading termination</b>."
            ]
