                "<b>Relation to SJF (Final 2020 Q48)</b>: SJF is simply a priority scheduling algorithm where the priority is the <b>inverse of the predicted next CPU burst time</b> (larger CPU burst = lower priority).",
                "<b>Problem & Solution (Slide 6.22 — Final 2020 Q49, Final 2024 Q6)</b>: The major problem with priority scheduling is <b>indefinite blocking (starvation)</b>, where low-priority processes may never execute. The solution is <b>aging</b>—gradually increasing the priority of processes that wait in the system for a long time.",
                "<b>Slide 6.23 Example (`P1(BT=10, Pr=3), P2(1, 1), P3(2, 4), P4(1, 5), P5(5, 2)` at time 0)</b>: Gantt chart is `P2 (0–1) → P5 (1–6) → P1 (6–16) → P3 (16–18) → P4 (18–19)`. Waiting times: `P1 = 6, P2 = 0, P3 = 16, P4 = 18, P5 = 1`. <b>Average WT = `(6 + 0 + 16 + 18 + 1) / 5 = 8.2 ms`</b>."
