              "<b>Cause of thrashing</b>: Thrashing occurs when processes are not allocated enough frames to hold their current active <b>locality</b> (<code>Σ size of locality &gt; total physical memory m</code>), causing constant page faults. Low CPU utilization tricks the long-term scheduler into admitting even more processes, worsening the page-fault spiral.<br><b>How the system detects thrashing</b>: By observing a sudden drop in CPU utilization combined with a spike in paging disk activity, or by monitoring each process's <b>Working-Set Size (<code>D = Σ WSS_i &gt; m</code>)</b> or <b>Page-Fault Frequency (PFF)</b> exceeding the upper bound.<br><b>How to eliminate thrashing</b>: (1) Use a <b>local (or priority) page-replacement algorithm</b> so a thrashing process cannot steal frames from other processes; (2) Use the <b>Working-Set model</b> or <b>Page-Fault Frequency (PFF)</b> to allocate enough frames to each active process; and (3) If total demand <code>D &gt; m</code>, <b>decrease the degree of multiprogramming</b> by suspending/swapping out one or more processes and redistributing their frames."
