          "notes": [
            {
              "h": "Step-by-Step Trace of the Readers-Writers Semaphore Queue (Midterm 2022 Q5)",
              "pts": [
                "Exam problems frequently ask you to trace the exact state of <code>readCount</code>, <code>mutex</code>, and <code>writeBlock</code> (<code>rw_mutex</code>) when readers and writers arrive in a specific sequence.",
                "<b>Initial State (Midterm 2022 Q5.1)</b>: <code>readCount = 0</code>, <code>mutex = 1</code>, <code>writeBlock = 1</code>.",
                "<b>Scenario A (Midterm 2022 Q5.2): A writer <code>W1</code> is currently updating the shared file, and three new readers <code>R1, R2, R3</code> arrive in order (with no other readers active)</b>:",
                "1. Because <code>W1</code> is inside its critical section, it has already executed <code>wait(writeBlock)</code>, so <code>writeBlock.value == 0</code>.",
                "2. <b><code>R1</code> arrives</b>: Executes <code>wait(mutex)</code> (succeeds, <code>mutex.value</code> becomes <code>0</code>), increments <code>readCount</code> from <code>0</code> to <code>1</code>, sees <code>readCount == 1</code>, and calls <code>wait(writeBlock)</code>. Since <code>writeBlock</code> is held by <code>W1</code>, <b><code>R1</code> is blocked on <code>writeBlock</code>'s queue while still holding <code>mutex</code></b>!",
                "3. <b><code>R2</code> and <code>R3</code> arrive</b>: Each calls <code>wait(mutex)</code> at the first line of the reader code. Since <code>R1</code> is holding <code>mutex</code>, <b>both <code>R2</code> and <code>R3</code> are blocked on <code>mutex</code>'s waiting queue</b>!",
                "4. When <code>W1</code> finishes and calls <code>signal(writeBlock)</code>, <code>R1</code> wakes up, calls <code>signal(mutex)</code>, and wakes <code>R2</code> (which then wakes <code>R3</code>), allowing all three readers to read concurrently.",
                "<b>Scenario B (Midterm 2022 Q5.3): Some readers are currently reading, and a new writer <code>W2</code> arrives</b>:",
                "• Because the first reader already executed <code>wait(writeBlock)</code> when <code>readCount</code> became <code>1</code>, <code>W2</code> immediately <b>blocks on <code>wait(writeBlock)</code></b>. Any subsequent readers that arrive while the existing readers are still reading will pass <code>wait(mutex)</code>, increment <code>readCount (&gt; 1)</code>, skip <code>wait(writeBlock)</code>, and read immediately—which is why <b>writers are subject to starvation</b> (Midterm 2022 Q5.4)!"
              ]
            },
            {
              "h": "Analyzing Incorrect Semaphore Sequences (Slide 5.38 & Final 2020/2024)",
              "pts": [
                "Always check the order and pairing of <code>wait(mutex)</code> and <code>signal(mutex)</code> when debugging concurrent code:",
                "• <b>Bug 1: <code>signal(mutex); ... CS ... wait(mutex);</code></b> → Increments <code>mutex</code> to <code>2</code> at the start, so multiple processes enter the critical section simultaneously (<b>Mutual Exclusion violated</b>).",
                "• <b>Bug 2: <code>wait(mutex); ... CS ... wait(mutex);</code></b> → Decrements <code>mutex</code> to <code>0</code> on entry and then to <code>-1</code> on exit, blocking the process forever while holding the lock (<b>Deadlock</b>).",
                "• <b>Bug 3: Producer calls <code>wait(mutex)</code> before <code>wait(empty)</code> in Bounded-Buffer</b> → If the buffer is full (<code>empty == 0</code>), the producer locks <code>mutex</code> and then sleeps on <code>empty</code>; when the consumer tries to consume an item to free space, it blocks forever on <code>wait(mutex)</code> (<b>Deadlock</b>)."
              ]
            }
          ],
