            "<b>Shortest-Job-First (SJF — Slides 6.13–6.14)</b>: Associates with each process the length of its <b>next CPU burst</b> and schedules the process with the smallest next CPU burst. <b>SJF is provably optimal</b>—it gives the <b>minimum average waiting time</b> for a given set of processes. Slide 6.14 Example (`P1=6, P2=8, P3=7, P4=3` at time 0): Schedule is `P4 (0–3) → P1 (3–9) → P3 (9–16) → P2 (16–24)`, giving Average WT = `(3 + 16 + 9 + 0) / 4 = 7 ms`.",
            "<b>Predicting Next CPU Burst via Exponential Averaging (Slides 6.15–6.17)</b>: Because the OS cannot know the exact future CPU burst length in batch/interactive systems, it predicts the next burst <code>τ_(n+1)</code> from the actual <code>n</code>-th burst <code>t_n</code> and previous prediction <code>τ_n</code> using <code>τ_(n+1) = α t_n + (1 − α) τ_n</code> (commonly <code>α = 1/2</code>). If <code>α = 0</code>, <code>τ_(n+1) = τ_n</code> (recent history does not count). If <code>α = 1</code>, <code>τ_(n+1) = t_n</code> (only the actual last CPU burst counts).",
            "<b>Non-Preemptive SJF vs. Preemptive SJF (SRTF — Slides 6.18–6.21)</b>: When a new process arrives in the ready queue with a CPU-burst length shorter than the <b>remaining time</b> of the currently executing process, <b>Shortest-Remaining-Time-First (SRTF)</b> preempts the current process, whereas non-preemptive SJF lets the current process finish.",
            "<b>Slide 6.19 vs. 6.20 Example (`P1(AT=0, BT=7), P2(AT=2, BT=4), P3(AT=4, BT=1), P4(AT=5, BT=4)`)</b>:",
            "• <b>Non-Preemptive SJF (Slide 6.19)</b>: `P1 (0–7) → P3 (7–8) → P2 (8–12) → P4 (12–16)`. Waiting times: `P1 = 0, P2 = 6, P3 = 3, P4 = 7`. <b>Average WT = `(0 + 6 + 3 + 7) / 4 = 4 ms`</b>.",
            "• <b>Preemptive SJF / SRTF (Slide 6.20)</b>: `P1 (0–2) → P2 (2–4) → P3 (4–5) → P2 (5–7) → P4 (7–11) → P1 (11–16)`. Waiting times: `P1 = 16 - (0 + 7) = 9`, `P2 = 7 - (2 + 4) = 1`, `P3 = 5 - (4 + 1) = 0`, `P4 = 11 - (5 + 4) = 2`. <b>Average WT = `(9 + 1 + 0 + 2) / 4 = 3 ms`</b>.",
