          "Selecting a CPU scheduling algorithm for a particular OS can be difficult. Briefly describe the four evaluation methods and show the advantages and disadvantages of each. (Final 2018 Q7 & Final 2024 Essay Q1)",
          "1. <b>Deterministic Modeling</b>: Takes a specific predetermined workload and computes exact metrics (such as average waiting time) for each algorithm. <i>Pros</i>: Simple, fast, gives exact numbers. <i>Cons</i>: Requires exact input data and results only apply to that specific workload.<br>2. <b>Queueing Models</b>: Models the computer as a network of servers and queues using probability distributions and <b>Little's Formula (<code>n = λ × W</code>)</b>. <i>Pros</i>: Analytical comparison across arrival/burst distributions. <i>Cons</i>: Complex algorithms require simplifying assumptions, limiting accuracy.<br>3. <b>Simulations</b>: Runs a programmed model of the computer system driven by random distributions or empirical <b>trace tapes</b>. <i>Pros</i>: Far more accurate than queueing models. <i>Cons</i>: Expensive to code and run, and trace tapes require large storage.<br>4. <b>Implementation</b>: Puts the actual algorithm into a real OS and tests it under live load. <i>Pros</i>: The only 100% accurate evaluation method. <i>Cons</i>: High engineering cost, disrupts users, and workloads adapt to the scheduler."
